          "text": "Leo, your direction is broadly correct. Confidence: high on the current behavior; moderate on the future decomposition until we settle scope and binding semantics.\n\n## How search works now\n\nIt does not invoke `grep`, ripgrep, SQL, or an index. It is effectively a bounded grep implemented directly in Node:\n\n1. Walk every directory under the owner’s Knowledge Space.\n2. Reject symlinks and unsafe paths.\n3. Consider only regular `.md` files.\n4. Read each file’s complete bytes.\n5. Decode strict UTF-8.\n6. Lowercase the query and each line.\n7. Use JavaScript `String.includes()` for literal substring matching.\n8. Return at most one result per file: the first matching line, adjacent lines, a bounded excerpt, path, and SHA-256 of the complete file bytes.\n9. Sort results by path and apply the requested result limit.\n\nSee [knowledge-filesystem.ts](/home/leon0399/Projects/llame/apps/api/src/knowledge/knowledge-filesystem.ts:142) and the current contract in [knowledge-tools/spec.md](/home/leon0399/Projects/llame/openspec/specs/knowledge-tools/spec.md:37).\n\nConsequences:\n\n- No regex.\n- No NFKC normalization.\n- No stemming or morphology.\n- No typo tolerance.\n- No paraphrase or cross-language retrieval.\n- No relevance ranking.\n- No caching.\n- Every search is proportional to the complete vault size.\n- It already returns only the first match plus surrounding context, not the whole file.\n\nFor your current 160-file, ~0.9 MiB vault, this is acceptable. It is not an architecture for large Knowledge Spaces.\n\n## The architecture I’d recommend\n\nDo not reuse chat search as a subsystem. Reuse its corpus-neutral primitives:\n\n- normalization;\n- deterministic chunking machinery;\n- content hashing;\n- FTS/trigram query construction;\n- reciprocal-rank fusion;\n- embedding registry/backends;\n- relevance evaluation.\n\nThe current `search/core` was explicitly structured for “chat now; knowledge/RAG later”: [text.ts](/home/leon0399/Projects/llame/apps/api/src/search/core/text.ts:1), [chunking.ts](/home/leon0399/Projects/llame/apps/api/src/search/core/chunking.ts:1), and [fusion.ts](/home/leon0399/Projects/llame/apps/api/src/search/core/fusion.ts:1).\n\nThe corpus lifecycle must remain separate:\n\n```text\nCanonical Knowledge files + Git history\n                  │\n                  ▼\n     change detection and reconciliation\n   watcher + periodic scan + explicit Git events\n                  │\n                  ▼\n       deterministic Markdown chunker\n   headings / paragraphs / lists / code blocks\n                  │\n                  ▼\n        node-local derived projection\n        ├── normalized text + FTS\n        ├── trigram index\n        └── versioned embeddings\n                  │\nquery ────────────┴──▶ ranked candidate legs\n                         │\n                         ▼\n                       RRF\n                         │\n                         ▼\n               live-file hash validation\n                         │\n                         ▼\n       bounded excerpts with line provenance\n```\n\nThe crucial difference from chat search: llame controls every chat mutation, but external editors can change files without notifying llame. Therefore:\n\n- Files/Git remain canonical.\n- Watchers improve freshness but cannot be trusted as the only mechanism.\n- A periodic manifest reconciliation catches missed events, restarts, renames, and deletions.\n- Projection rows are keyed by space, path, chunker version, and content hash.\n- Before returning top results, reopen the live file and verify its hash. A stale index may generate candidates but must not become evidence.\n- Indexes and embeddings are local derived state and are never synchronized between Nodes. Each Node rebuilds them from the files it possesses.\n\nThat preserves the principle already in [VISION.md](/home/leon0399/Projects/llame/VISION.md:51): human-readable files are canonical; indexes and embeddings are rebuildable projections.\n\n## Search pipeline\n\nA Knowledge-specific Markdown chunker should preserve semantic boundaries:\n\n- heading hierarchy;\n- paragraphs;\n- list blocks;\n- fenced code;\n- source line range;\n- controlled adjacent overlap.\n\nEach chunk would carry roughly:\n\n```text\nowner_id\nknowledge_space_id\nrelative_path\nfile_content_hash\nchunk_ordinal\nchunker_version\nheading_path\nstart_line / end_line\noriginal_content\nnormalized_content\nchunk_hash\nindexed_at\n```\n\nRetrieval then uses three legs:\n\n1. Full-text search for exact terms and phrases.\n2. Trigrams for typos, fragments, identifiers, and filenames.\n3. Multilingual vectors for paraphrases and cross-language queries.\n\nFuse ranks with RRF. Do not mix raw FTS, trigram, and cosine scores; they are incomparable. This is the same principle planned for chat [#196](https://github.com/leon0399/llame/issues/196) and [#197](https://github.com/leon0399/llame/issues/197).\n\nStart with exact pgvector scans. HNSW, Qdrant, or OpenSearch are unjustified until measurements show Postgres failing.\n\n## Search tool evolution\n\nThe future search result should be passage-oriented:\n\n```ts\n{\n  knowledgeSpaceId,\n  path,\n  headingPath,\n  startLine,\n  endLine,\n  excerpt,\n  fileContentHash,\n  matchedBy: [\"fts\", \"trigram\", \"vector\"]\n}\n```\n\nLikely behavior:\n\n- return several best passages, not merely the first literal line;\n- cap repeated passages from one file so one long document cannot dominate;\n- group or deduplicate adjacent chunks;\n- return line ranges and heading context;\n- do not return the full file;\n- revalidate the file hash before returning the excerpt;\n- if the file changed, discard the stale candidate and queue reindexing.\n\nFor vector-only matches, there is no literal matching text to highlight. The bounded chunk itself is the evidence.\n\n## Read tool evolution\n\nCurrent `knowledge_read` reads a complete file and rejects anything above 64 KiB. That is why your 73 KiB game-design document cannot be read despite being searchable.\n\nA better contract is approximately:\n\n```ts\nknowledge_read({\n  knowledgeSpaceId,\n  path,\n  startLine?: number,\n  lineCount?: number,\n  expectedContentHash?: string\n})\n```\n\nReturn:\n\n```ts\n{\n  path,\n  startLine,\n  endLine,\n  totalLines,\n  content,\n  fileContentHash\n}\n```\n\nImportant details:\n\n- Small files may still default to a complete read.\n- Large files default to a bounded first range.\n- Search results feed their path, line range, and hash into read.\n- `expectedContentHash` prevents search→read races: if the file changed, return `knowledge_content_changed` and search again.\n- The implementation can scan/hash a bounded-size file while returning only selected lines, separating input size from model-output size.\n- No arbitrary host paths or generic filesystem access.\n\n## Multiple Knowledge Spaces\n\nThis is a separate resource-model change, not merely another search filter.\n\nThe vision direction is sound:\n\n- `knowledge_space_id` is identity.\n- Display names are non-unique metadata.\n- Two spaces with identical names but different IDs remain separate.\n- Same ID means replicas of one logical space and uses Git reconciliation.\n- Absorbing one space into another is an explicit semantic workflow and never deletes the source.\n- Index rows always carry `knowledge_space_id`.\n- Each Node builds its own projection for the replicas locally available there.\n\nA portable Knowledge Space identity probably needs a resource manifest or equivalent portable envelope. The current hosted database ID alone is insufficient for raw vault export/import: a Git repository has no inherent globally stable resource ID. An explicit “fork this space” operation must mint a new ID; an ordinary replica/import must preserve the existing ID.\n\n## Likely issue decomposition\n\nThis is too large for one proposal or PR:\n\n1. Multiple-space identity, portable manifest, and Chat/Run binding.\n2